  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nShowers continue across the interior this afternoon into early\nevening as an embedded wave in cyclonic flow passes overhead. It\nwill also bring breezy conditions across southern ME through the\nafternoon.\n\nOvernight, gusts slacken but there will remain a breeze as wave\ncompletes its passing. There will be the continued chance for\nshowers across much of NH through tonight, but these should be\nout of the area by Sunday morning.\n\nSunday and Monday will be drier, but remain breezy in proximity\nto tight pressure gradient to the east. Less clouds Sunday night\nbrings the possibility for radiational cooling in the valleys,\nbut higher, more exposed locations could see continued breeze\nthat negates further overnight cooling.\n\nWith the eastward exit of low pressure into Tuesday, comes a\ndrier continental airmass from Quebec. Temperatures will have\nwarmed from the weekend, but only into the upper 70s to around\n80. Lower daytime relative humidity values could allow for a \nefficient drying day, but the next chance of rain will be\nadvancing towards the area mid to late week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nEnsemble MSLP has a sub-1000 mb low pressure moving thru Quebec\nnorth of the Gaspe Peninsula. A cold front will trail that low \npressure, and most of the weather next week will be centered \naround its passage. A modest southwest LLJ, on the order of 40 \nto 50 knots, will develop Wed, but conditions at the surface \nlook stable enough that I am not anticipating significant wind \ngusts from it. The southwest winds will transport more moisture \ninto the region though, so dewpoints will climb into the 50s \nacross the region, and may even push the 60s in parts of the \nsouthern forecast area. While overall the forecast soundings are\nlooking pretty stable, this may be enough for some nominal \ninstability by afternoon. That is concern because there will be \nplenty of shear thanks to the aforementioned LLJ. CSU machine \nlearning severe probabilities are showing a roughly 5 percent \nsevere thunderstorm risk locally Wed. That seems reasonable, not\nsomething I plan to highlight in the forecast grids just yet, \nbut worth mentioning in discussions/briefings.\n\nThe precip itself will be showery in nature along the cold \nfront. So I do not anticipate widespread significant rainfall. \nThis is yet another high Pop/low QPF type event, which is more \nor less what the NBM is forecasting at this time.\n\nFinally once the front crosses the area, winds shift to \nnorthwesterly as cold air advection commences. On Thu as mixing \nheights increase that will lead to a gusty day. At this time \nforecast soundings over much of the area indicate that gusts \naround 30 mph are likely. However a few spots may push 40 mph \nacross southern NH and coastal western Maine. Once that cooler \nairmass settles into the region, Fri night and Sat night may be \nchilly across many spots. Some widespread 40s are likely, and a \nthe northern valley cold spots may be flirting with upper 30s.",
